  "story_id": "S022",
  "title": "Chp1 loss recurrently activates an SREBP2 cholesterol-biosynthesis program across neurons",
  "status": "grounded",
  "rejection_reason": null,
  "exact_observed_result": "Chp1 knockdown induced canonical SREBP2 sterol genes in 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut and 155 MB Glut, including Insig1, Hmgcs1, Msmo1, and Cyp51. Direct Srebf2 loss moved the same module downward, while Dctn1, Dync1h1, Htt, Mib1, Agps, and Far1 supplied narrower same-direction or contextual sterol signatures.",
  "biological_interpretation": "CHP1 depletion appears to be sensed as lipid/ER-homeostatic stress that converges on a compensatory SREBP2 cholesterol-demand program. The state also connects to dynein/dynactin and endolysosomal transport perturbations, but Hap1, Srebf1, Tubb3, and Usf1 are negative comparators rather than equal members.",
